---
import { curlSnippet, producerConfig } from '@pbcp/client';
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { HEAD_REPO_URL, REPO_URL, href } from '../config';
import { connectors } from '../lib/connectors';
import { rfcSections } from '../lib/markdown';

const sec = rfcSections();
const rfc = (n: string) => `${href('/rfc/')}#${sec.get(n)}`;
// The same placeholders as the connectors' READMEs; the text below is what the packages produce.
const placeholders = { api: '<head url>', owner: '<owner>', token: '<token>' };
const config = JSON.stringify(producerConfig(placeholders), null, 2);
const curl = curlSnippet(placeholders);
const withSetup = connectors().filter((c) => c.setup);
---
<Base title="Producers" path="/producers/" description="What a producer needs to reach a head, a first card with curl, and where each connector's setup is.">
  <h1>Producers</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    A producer is anything that sends cards: an agent, a script, a mail reader. It needs three values from the head,
    and an HTTP client.
  </p>

  <h2>Three values</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The head URL.</strong> Where the head serves the producer operations: its <code>api</code>
      (<a href={rfc('7.5')}>§7.5</a>), or else the URL its description is read from. <code>GET</code> on the origin
      returns the description.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The owner.</strong> The person the head stores cards for, the <code>owner</code> in its description. Every
      card is addressed to it in <code>to_person</code> (<a href={rfc('10')}>§10</a>).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A token.</strong> Issued by the head and bound to one namespace root: every id the producer sends starts
      with that root (<a href={rfc('12')}>§12</a>). How a head issues tokens is up to the head; the
      <a href={HEAD_REPO_URL}>reference head</a> mints them on its settings page, one per producer, shown once and
      revocable.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>As the environment the repository's connectors read, from <code>producerConfig</code> in <code>@pbcp/client</code>:</p>
  <pre><code>{config}</code></pre>

  <h2>A first card</h2>
  <p>
    Submit is a <code>PUT</code> to the card's id (<a href={rfc('7.1')}>§7.1</a>). This sends the example card from
    <a href={rfc('3')}>§3</a>, addressed to the owner. Its id is under the root <code>hermes</code>, so it needs a token
    bound to that root. It is the output of <code>curlSnippet</code> in <code>@pbcp/client</code>, which takes any other
    card too.
  </p>
  <pre><code>{curl}</code></pre>
  <p>
    The head answers <code>201</code> with the stored card, now carrying the fields it owns. The same command again
    answers <code>200</code> and changes nothing: the id names the card (<a href={rfc('5')}>§5</a>).
  </p>

  <h2>Reading the decision</h2>
  <p>
    The person decides in the head. A producer reads decisions with <code>GET /changes?after=</code>
    (<a href={rfc('7.2')}>§7.2</a>): every card in its namespace that changed after a cursor, in order. It keeps the
    returned <code>next</code> and passes it back, and acts on each decision in a way that is safe to repeat.
    <a href={`${REPO_URL}/tree/main/packages/client`}><code>@pbcp/client</code></a>'s <code>runProducer</code> is that
    loop with a durable cursor; <a href={`${REPO_URL}/tree/main/packages/mcp`}><code>@pbcp/mcp</code></a> gives an agent
    the same operations as tools.
  </p>

  <h2>Connectors</h2>
  <p>
    Each connector's README has a Setup section with the three values in place and whatever else it needs. It is
    generated from the connector's own <code>connect()</code>, so it matches the code.
  </p>
  <ul class="links">
    {withSetup.map((c) => <li><a href={c.setup}><code>{c.dir}</code> setup</a> <span class="muted">— {c.settings.filter((s) => !s.startsWith('PBCP_')).slice(0, 4).join(', ') || 'no further settings'}</span></li>)}
  </ul>
  <p>The <a href={href('/connectors/')}>connectors page</a> lists every connector and its settings.</p>

  <h2>Next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={rfc('11.1')}>§11.1</a>, the producer checklist.</li>
    <li><a href={href('/rfc/')}>The RFC</a>, for threads, supersession and urgency.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
